<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  What this application asks the phone for, kept where a regeneration cannot
  reach it.

  `gen/apple/sync-mobile_iOS/Info.plist` is written whole by `pnpm tauri ios
  init`, so a key added there survives until the next person runs the command
  the trap in AGENTS.md tells them to run. The CLI merges `Info.plist` and
  `Info.ios.plist` from beside `tauri.conf.json` into the generated one, and
  this file is that: the second name rather than the first, because it says out
  loud that these keys are a phone's.

  The string is not a note to a developer. iOS shows it inside the alert that
  asks, so it is written to the person holding the phone, and it names the one
  thing the camera is for here.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Sync reads the pairing code your computer is showing, so that this phone can reach it. The camera is used for nothing else.</string>
	<!--
	  Asked for because the computer is usually on the same network as the
	  phone, and that is the short way to it: without this the connection can
	  only go out to a relay and back, which is slower and needs the internet
	  to reach a machine in the same room. iOS refuses local addresses outright
	  until somebody has agreed to this, and refuses them silently.
	-->
	<!--
	  Said once here rather than answered by hand on every upload. The channel
	  is encrypted by its transport — QUIC, with the ends authenticated — and
	  nothing in this application implements cryptography of its own, which is
	  exactly the exemption. Left out, App Store Connect asks the question again
	  for each build and holds the build in TestFlight until somebody answers.
	-->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Sync reaches your computer directly when it is on the same network as this phone. Nothing is sent anywhere else.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
